course: dictionary
lesson: 10774
title: Vaccinate and protection
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A vaccine is given to a person or animal, usually by injection. It helps the body learn how to fight a disease before the person catches it. To vaccinate means to give that vaccine. Look at the context: «Hundreds of dogs in the zone come from pets left behind in 1986; scientists study their DNA, and volunteers vaccinate and feed them.» Feeding and vaccinating are both things people do to care for animals, so vaccinate is a kind of care.
explain: The sentence «Kittens stay inside until they are vaccinated, neutered and old enough» gives a clue too. Being outside brings cars, dogs and fights, so vaccinated must be something done to protect a kitten from illness before it goes out. Ask yourself: what would fit here? Then swap in your meaning, "given a vaccine so the body can fight a disease", and check it still makes sense.
explain: The words around a word often show its purpose. In «Measles… spreads again wherever fewer people are vaccinated», the clue is that fewer vaccinated people means more spreading. So being vaccinated is what keeps a disease from spreading.
fact: To vaccinate means to give a person or animal a vaccine.
fact: A vaccine is usually given by injection, which is a shot with a needle.
fact: A vaccine helps the body learn to fight a disease before it catches that disease.
fact: Other forms of the word: vaccinates, vaccinated, vaccinating; the noun vaccination is the act of giving a vaccine.
fact: Useful phrases: «get vaccinated» and «be vaccinated against something», such as measles, polio or flu.
exercise: «Kittens stay inside until they are vaccinated, neutered and old enough.» What does vaccinated mean here? | *Given a vaccine so the body can fight a disease | Fed and cleaned every day | Kept warm in a box | Taken to the vet for a check-up only
exercise: «Scientists study their DNA, and volunteers vaccinate and feed them.» Find and copy the word that means to give a vaccine. | feed | study | volunteers | *vaccinate
exercise: «Measles… spreads again wherever fewer people are vaccinated.» What happens when fewer people are vaccinated? | Vaccines become cheaper | *Measles can spread again | Doctors stop working | Measles disappears forever
test: A vaccine is usually given how? | Breathed in through the nose | *By injection, with a small needle | Rubbed onto the skin like cream | Swallowed as a sweet syrup
test: Which word is the noun for the act of giving a vaccine? | *vaccination | vaccinates | vaccinated | vaccinating
exam: «Only trained, vaccinated people should handle them.» Why are the vaccinated people the ones who should handle the bats? | Because bats only trust people who are vaccinated | Because they have a vaccine and the bats cannot hurt them | *Because they are protected against the bats' rare virus | Because they are the fastest runners
